# 5. TARGET ARCHITECTURE AND ENGINEERING CONTRACT

Implement the following direction while adapting to the real repository.

## A. CENTRAL AUTHORITATIVE SERVER

The server is the source of truth for shared business data.

Client devices must never independently decide the authoritative values of:
- Stock.
- Invoice totals.
- Payment state.
- Debt balances.
- Transaction status.
- Financial journal state.
- License entitlements.

Clients submit validated commands.

The server validates and commits them.

The server returns authoritative results.

## B. TRANSACTIONAL FINANCIAL INTEGRITY

All financial operations must be transactionally safe.

Use database transactions and appropriate constraints to ensure:
- An invoice cannot be partially committed.
- An invoice cannot be duplicated through retries.
- Stock deductions cannot be applied twice.
- Payments cannot be partially applied without a defined state.
- Returns are linked to the appropriate original transaction.
- Accounting entries remain consistent with the operation.
- Failure midway cannot leave corrupt financial state.

Never use client-calculated totals as unquestioned authority.

## C. SECURITY BOUNDARIES

Implement real server-side controls.

Every sensitive route and operation must be evaluated for:
- Authentication.
- Authorization.
- Role and permission.
- Input validation.
- Rate limits.
- Resource ownership.
- Session lifecycle.
- Audit requirements.
- Secure error handling.

No critical security decision may depend exclusively on client-side JavaScript.

## D. LAN SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH

All connected devices must share the same authoritative database through the server.

The architecture must support:
- Multiple simultaneous clients.
- Accurate shared inventory.
- Accurate invoice state.
- Safe concurrent transactions.
- Event-based synchronization.
- Reconnection.
- Catch-up.
- Device authentication.
- Responsive interfaces.
- Graceful degraded network behavior.

## E. DATA OWNERSHIP

The customer must own and be able to recover their business data.

The licensing system must never be used to hold customer data hostage.

Reading, exporting, and recovery must be treated as customer-protection capabilities.

